  "Question": "If \\(m\\gt0\\) and the points \\((m,3)\\) and \\((1,m)\\) lie on a line with slope \\(m,\\) then \\(m=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\sqrt2\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\sqrt3\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\sqrt5\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The slope formula connects the two given points to the unknown \\(m\\). First, \\(m=1\\) is impossible: the points would be \\((1,3)\\) and \\((1,1)\\), whose connecting line is vertical and has no finite slope.\n\nFor \\(m\\ne1\\), the slope condition is\n\\[\n\\frac{3-m}{m-1}=m.\n\\]\nMultiplying by the nonzero denominator gives\n\\[\n3-m=m(m-1)=m^2-m,\n\\]\nso \\(m^2=3\\). The two roots are \\(\\pm\\sqrt3\\), and \\(m>0\\) leaves \\(m=\\sqrt3\\). This value is not \\(1\\), so the preceding steps are reversible and it satisfies the original slope condition. Therefore the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt3}\\), choice C.",
